/-
# Composition factors of groups of genus g (the Guralnick-Thompson conjecture)

UnsolvedMath seed problem ALG-025 (The Guralnick-Thompson Conjecture): what are the composition
factors of finite groups appearing in genus-0 systems? Guralnick and Thompson conjectured that,
apart from cyclic and alternating groups, only finitely many simple groups occur as composition
factors of monodromy groups of covers of the sphere by curves of genus `g`.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.IsSubnormal
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.SpecificGroups.Alternating

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.GuralnickThompsonUnsolvedMathALG025Wanted

/-!
Source: UnsolvedMath seed problem ALG-025 (The Guralnick-Thompson Conjecture); conjecture of R. M.
Guralnick and J. G. Thompson, Finite groups of genus zero, J. Algebra 131 (1990), 303-341, as
recalled in K. Magaard, S. Shpectorov and G. Wang, Generating sets of affine groups of low genus,
arXiv:1108.4833, Section 3. UnsolvedMath record UM-00001189 (`ALG-025`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath
v1.8.0.

Guralnick and Thompson define `E*(g)` as the set of isomorphism types of composition factors of the
monodromy groups `Mon(X, φ)` of meromorphic functions `φ` on compact connected Riemann surfaces `X`
of genus `g`, other than the alternating groups `A_n` (`n > 4`) and the cyclic groups `ℤ/p`, and
conjecture that `E*(g)` is finite for every `g` (Magaard-Shpectorov-Wang, Section 3). Monodromy
groups are generated by genus `g` systems (Definition 11 there): `x₁, …, x_r ∈ S_n` with
`x₁ ⋯ x_r = 1` generating a transitive subgroup, with `∑ ind xᵢ = 2 (n + g - 1)` (Riemann-Hurwitz),
where `ind x = n - #orbits of ⟨x⟩`; conversely every genus `g` system comes from a cover (Riemann's
existence theorem, Theorem 12 there). The entries are taken nonidentity, as branch cycles are;
identity entries change neither the product, the index sum nor the generated group. `permIndex`
computes `ind` from the cycle type. The target is stated for every genus `g`, so the record's
genus-0 question is the case `g = 0`. A composition factor is a simple quotient of a subnormal
subgroup (`Subgroup.IsSubnormal`); finiteness of a set of isomorphism types of finite simple groups
is stated as a bound `N(g)` on their orders. The record's question asks which groups occur; only the
finiteness statement, which the resolution proves, is formalized, not an explicit list of the
exceptional groups.
-/

/-- The index `ind σ` of a permutation `σ` of `Fin n`: `n` minus the number of orbits of `⟨σ⟩` on
`Fin n`, that is, the sum of `ℓ - 1` over the cycle lengths `ℓ` of `σ` (fixed points contribute
`0`). -/
def permIndex {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) : ℕ :=
  (σ.cycleType.map fun ℓ => ℓ - 1).sum

/-- A genus `g` system of degree `n` (Magaard-Shpectorov-Wang, Definition 11, with nonidentity
entries): nonidentity permutations `x₀, …, x_{r-1}` of `Fin n` with `x₀ ⋯ x_{r-1} = 1` that
generate a transitive subgroup of `S_n` and satisfy the Riemann-Hurwitz relation
`∑ ind xᵢ = 2 (n + g - 1)`. -/
def IsGenusSystem (g : ℕ) {n r : ℕ} (x : Fin r → Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) : Prop :=
  (∀ i, x i ≠ 1) ∧ (List.ofFn x).prod = 1 ∧
    (∀ a b : Fin n, ∃ σ ∈ Subgroup.closure (Set.range x), σ a = b) ∧
    ∑ i, permIndex (x i) = 2 * (n + g - 1)

/-- `S` is a composition factor of `G`: a simple group that is a quotient of a subnormal subgroup of
`G` (a composition series of `G` can be refined through the subnormal subgroup and the kernel). -/
def IsCompositionFactor (G : Type) [Group G] (S : Type) [Group S] : Prop :=
  IsSimpleGroup S ∧ ∃ (H : Subgroup G) (φ : H →* S), H.IsSubnormal ∧ Function.Surjective φ

/-- The Guralnick-Thompson conjecture: for every genus `g`, the simple groups that are neither
cyclic nor alternating and occur as composition factors of groups generated by genus `g` systems
form a finite set of isomorphism types; equivalently, their orders are bounded by some `N(g)`. -/
def guralnickThompsonConjecture : Prop :=
  ∀ g : ℕ, ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ (n r : ℕ) (x : Fin r → Equiv.Perm (Fin n)), IsGenusSystem g x →
    ∀ (S : Type) [Group S], IsCompositionFactor (Subgroup.closure (Set.range x)) S →
      ¬ IsCyclic S → (∀ m : ℕ, IsEmpty (S ≃* alternatingGroup (Fin m))) → Nat.card S ≤ N

/--
Resolved true: Frohardt and Magaard prove the Guralnick-Thompson conjecture that for every `g ≥ 0`
the set `E*(g)` of composition factors, neither cyclic nor alternating, of monodromy groups of genus
`g` covers of the sphere is finite; through Riemann's existence theorem these monodromy groups are
the groups generated by genus `g` systems. Source: UnsolvedMath seed problem ALG-025 (The
Guralnick-Thompson Conjecture); conjecture of R. M. Guralnick and J. G. Thompson, Finite groups of
genus zero, J. Algebra 131 (1990), 303-341, as recalled in K. Magaard, S. Shpectorov and G. Wang,
Generating sets of affine groups of low genus, arXiv:1108.4833, Section 3; resolution: D. Frohardt
and K. Magaard, Composition factors of monodromy groups, Ann. of Math. (2) 154 (2001), 327-345,
abstract: the authors prove the 1990 conjecture of Guralnick and Thompson on composition factors of
monodromy groups, via Riemann's existence theorem and primitive permutation groups; the
combinatorial form (genus g systems, Definition 11) and the attribution of the proof to Frohardt and
Magaard are also in K. Magaard, S. Shpectorov and G. Wang, Generating sets of affine groups of low
genus, arXiv:1108.4833, Section 3, https://doi.org/10.2307/3062099.
-/
public theorem_wanted guralnickThompsonConjecture_holds : guralnickThompsonConjecture

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.GuralnickThompsonUnsolvedMathALG025Wanted
